WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.600
The OWASP Top 10 has developed into&nbsp;
an international security standard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:08.480
used by organizations across the globe.&nbsp;
But even organizations that use OWASP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:14.560
still end up as victims of cyberattack. So what&nbsp;
is the OWASP Top 10 and how should you be using it?

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:29.840
What do you need to know so it becomes your friend?

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:39.360
OWASP, or the Open Web Application Security&nbsp;
Project, was launched in 2003 to improve the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:46.400
security of software, notably web applications.&nbsp;
The OWASP Top 10 is a standard awareness document&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:51.360
that represents a broad consensus about the&nbsp;
most critical security risks to web applications.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:57.200
Nearly all involved with OWASP are volunteers&nbsp;
and it is not affiliated with any technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.800
company. This lack of commercial pressure&nbsp;
cements OWASP's claim of "unbiased, practical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.360
and cost-effective information about&nbsp;
application security." Or does it?

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.400
OWASP uses a community of&nbsp;
open source software projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.600
hundreds of local chapters worldwide, tens of&nbsp;
thousands of members and training conferences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:26.240
to compile their list of Top 10 risks. Data and&nbsp;
documentation is fed into a GitHub repository&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:31.920
managed by OWASP after the Top 10 core team&nbsp;
release a data call. Industry contributors are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:38.000
also encouraged to participate in a survey. Once&nbsp;
the data is collected in GitHub, it is normalized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:44.240
and analyzed. Surveys are collected and reviewed. A&nbsp;
draft Top 10 list is then made using eight risks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:49.760
taken from the data and two from the survey. The&nbsp;
draft is released publicly and the editing process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:55.680
is recorded in GitHub. Once a public consensus&nbsp;
has been reached, the OWASP Top 10 is released.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:04.480
Last updated in 2017, the new list&nbsp;
released on 24 September 2021&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:09.840
featured changes in category order, category name&nbsp;
changes as well as some entirely new categories.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.760
Let's take a look at the 2021 list and explain&nbsp;
what the Top 10 categories actually mean.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:24.000
Broken Access Control - up from number&nbsp;
five - has taken the top spot in 2021.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.720
This is an important change as it replaced&nbsp;
injection - largely seen as the most popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:34.320
form of risk exploited by cybercriminals.&nbsp;
Broken Access Control allows attackers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:39.280
bypass authorization safeguards and perform&nbsp;
tasks as if they were privileged users.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.880
Put simply, broken access control gives an&nbsp;
attacker the ability to expose sensitive data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:51.440
modify or destroy data, or perform pretty much any&nbsp;
network function. It is the holy grail of exploits.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:00.160
Cryptographic Failures - previously called&nbsp;
Sensitive Data Exposure - is in at number two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:06.080
from number three. Cryptographic Failures&nbsp;
signifies a renewed focus by OWASP on failures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:11.600
related to cryptography. In essence, this category&nbsp;
exploits information transferred over secure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:16.080
communication between two parties, which often&nbsp;
provides an attacker with useful information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:20.080
in the post-exploitation phase, like&nbsp;
how to maintain persistent access.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:29.040
Injection - previously known as Cross-site Scripting&nbsp;
and considered the king of vulnerabilities -&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:34.560
slid down to number three. Injection exploits&nbsp;
a vulnerable computer program by introducing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:40.880
code that changes the way a program executes. For&nbsp;
example, by injecting code into a web application&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.040
an attacker can steal authentication&nbsp;
cookies and use them on other online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:48.960
services, tricking these services into&nbsp;
thinking that the attacker is you.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:58.080
Insecure Design - a new category for the&nbsp;
2021 OWASP Top 10 - is in at number four. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.960
was included as a firm call for developers to&nbsp;
include more security patterns and principles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:08.080
by design. This is an important consideration&nbsp;
when including a new application into your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:11.920
network infrastructure, especially given&nbsp;
the supply chain attack on SolarWinds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:15.120
and the effect it had on&nbsp;third-party service providers.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:23.840
Security Misconfiguration - in at number five,&nbsp;
up from number six - was present in 90%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:28.720
of applications tested, not surprising&nbsp;
given the rise in highly configurable software.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:34.000
Security Misconfiguration is a failure to&nbsp;
implement all the security controls for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.440
server or web application, or implementing&nbsp;
security controls but with errors. Everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:44.400
makes mistakes and security misconfigurations&nbsp;
occur through a broad range of human errors:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:49.040
misinterpreting a system implementation;&nbsp;
not changing default logging credentials;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:55.840
a lack of computer skills; or, mistakes&nbsp;
made under time pressure, for example.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.560
Vulnerable and Outdated Components -&nbsp;
previously known as Using Components&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:05.600
with Known Vulnerabilities - is up at number&nbsp;
six, from number nine. By OWASP's own admission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:09.280
this category is difficult to test, but&nbsp;
featured highly in the community survey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:14.400
therefore contributing to its recent rank.&nbsp;
Vulnerable and Outdated Components includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.280
operating systems, web or application&nbsp;
servers, database management systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:24.160
apis, and all components runtime environments&nbsp;
and libraries that are vulnerable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:29.680
unsupported, or out of date. An adversary only&nbsp;
needs to find one vulnerable component to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:34.720
compromise a system. The problem is approximately&nbsp;
18,000 new vulnerabilities are found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:38.560
each year, so protecting against known&nbsp;
vulnerabilities is an ongoing process.

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:47.280
A new name given to Broken Authentication,&nbsp;
Identification and Authentication Failures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:51.840
comes in at number seven, down from number two - a&nbsp;
good sign that standardized frameworks are helping.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:56.000
broken authentication referred&nbsp;to the compromise of 
passwords,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:02.240
keys, session tokens, user account information&nbsp;
and other details to assume user identities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:06.800
The new position of Identification and&nbsp;
Authentication Failures shows that procedures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:11.760
like MFA are integral in authenticating key&nbsp;
information, like usernames and passwords.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:20.240
Another new category, Software and Data Integrity&nbsp;
Failures comes in at number eight, focusing on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:27.520
software updates, critical data, CI/CD pipelines&nbsp;
and includes Insecure Desterilization from 2017.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:32.320
This new category relates to code and&nbsp;
infrastructure that does not protect against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:38.480
integrity violations. An example of this is where&nbsp;
an application, like Wordpress, relies on plugins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:43.760
from untrusted sources one compromise plugin&nbsp;
can give an attacker access to your application.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:51.440
Previously categorized as Insufficient&nbsp;
Logging and Monitoring, Security Logging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:53.600
and Monitoring Failures comes in at number nine,

00:06:53.600 --> 00:07:01.276
up from number 10. Although admittedly hard to&nbsp;
test for due to a lack of representation in CVE/CSS data,

00:07:01.276 --> 00:07:04.880
Security Logging and Monitoring Failures&nbsp;
can seriously impact visibility and forensics.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:14.160
Lastly, at number 10, Server-side Request Forgery&nbsp;
is another new addition due to its position as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:18.960
number one in the community survey, indicating&nbsp;
strong feedback from the security community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:24.880
that it is an important risk but not illustrated&nbsp;
in the data collected. Server-side Request Forgery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:29.840
is a type of exploit where an attacker abuses the&nbsp;
functionality of a server causing it to access or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:34.400
manipulate information that would otherwise&nbsp;
not be directly accessible to the attacker.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:43.520
The OWASP Top 10 is the go-to listing for anyone&nbsp;
concerned with raising their awareness of top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:48.480
risks. The addition of survey considerations&nbsp;
as well as pure statistical data means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:54.240
OWASP does not solely rely on data collected by&nbsp;
automated tests. Where possible, the categories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:59.680
are ranked given three main considerations&nbsp;
exploitability likelihood and technical impact. 

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:05.840
However, OWASP themselves claim that it is a&nbsp;
baseline - a "pseudo standard" even - for compliance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:10.960
education and vendor tools. As an awareness&nbsp;
document, it is regarded as the bare minimum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:17.200
reference for standards of coding, code reviews,&nbsp;
peer reviews, tool support and penetration testing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:23.360
Being focused on AppSec means that many of the Top&nbsp;
10 findings cannot be easily tested for in-house.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:28.640
Being a voluntary service means that participants&nbsp;
releasing information publicly do so theoretically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:34.880
without the experience of a real world attack. If&nbsp;
you're keen to hear more about OWASP's Top 10 list,&nbsp;

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:39.840
keep an eye out for our next video which compares&nbsp;
OWASP's findings with our own list of Top 10 risks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:45.487
The risk is real. Defend with Cyber Citadel.

